  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 AM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES WV imagery shows the mid level trough now over \nthe Upper Mississippi River Valley with RAP analysis plotting the \nassociated 1004 mb low now over northern IL. A cold front is \npressing south over the CWA early this morning while the abundant \nmoisture from yesterday begins to exit. With instability falling and \ndeep moisture dissipating, shower activity is tapering down and \nthunder has ceased. That said, isolated to scattered showers closer \nto the low over the east half of the UP and northerly upslope \ndrizzle are both possible today as a secondary cold front progresses \nsouth overhead. Low level moisture will remain high, possibly \nresulting in patchy fog, mainly over the east half this morning. \nCooler temps today in the 60s and 70s, coldest near eastern Lake \nSuperior. As the UP dries out for tonight under weak high pressure \nridging, colder temps are expected in the mid 40s to upper 50s, \ncoldest interior west.\n\nNotably drier air can be expected for Sunday into Monday with \ndewpoints in the 50s, resulting in more comfortable conditions. \nTemperatures will be in the mid 60s to 70s on Sunday and upper 70s \nto low 80s on Monday (close to seasonal averages).\n\nOn Tuesday humidity will begin increasing ahead of an approaching \ncold front. Showers and thunderstorms may accompany the front on \nTuesday but the risk for organized convection remains uncertain at \nthis point in time, with global models showing lower instability and \nshear. It will fall back into the 70s for Wednesday behind the front \nwith a rebound again on Thursday along with a slight chance for \nthunderstorms.",
